\title{\strong{ZIG-ZIG}}
\date{2026-11-25T22:36:19Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{If #{x} and #{p} are either both left children or both right children, then do two successive rotations: first rotate around #{g \to p} then around #{p \to x}.

\diagram{600}{*}{\verb<<<|
import Diagrams.Prelude hiding (Empty)
import SplayDia
import Diagrams.TwoD.Layout.Tree

dia = frame 0.1 . hsep 1 . map centerXY $    -- $
  [ drawSplay (nd 'g' (nd 'p' (nd 'x' (sub 'A') (sub 'B')) (sub 'C')) (sub 'D'))
  , arrowV unitX
  , drawSplay (nd 'p' (nd 'x' (sub 'A') (sub 'B')) (nd 'g' (sub 'C') (sub 'D')))
  , arrowV unitX
  , drawSplay (nd 'x' (sub 'A') (nd 'p' (sub 'B') (nd 'g' (sub 'C') (sub 'D'))))
  ]
<<<}

  \p{Some things to note:}
  \ul{
    \li{This rule applies \em{anywhere} in the tree, unlike \strong{ZIG} which only applies when #{x} is at a depth of #{1}. After applying \strong{ZIG-ZIG}, #{x} has moved two levels closer to the root.}
    \li{Like \strong{ZIG}, this takes constant time, since we just do two rotations.}
    \li{Again, in the picture #{x} and #{p} are illustrated as both being left children, but there is an analogous symmetric case when #{x} and #{p} are both \em{right} children.}
  }

  }
